Here's an example of lightly obfuscated code that's still readable for humans while being harder for naive static analysis tools to parse:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

static const char *k = "kernel";
static const char *r = "rootkit";

static void x(char *s, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        s[i] ^= 0x55;
}

int main(void) {
    char a[16] = { 0x4b, 0x65, 0x72, 0x6e, 0x65, 0x6c, 0x00, 0x00,
                   0x52, 0x6f, 0x6f, 0x74, 0x6b, 0x69, 0x74, 0x00 };
    x(a, 16);
    printf("%s %s\n", a, k);
    return 0;
}
```

This uses simple XOR obfuscation, string splitting, and minor control-flow tricks. For stronger obfuscation, you could add:

- Polymorphic code generation
- Dynamic API resolution
- Anti-debugging checks
- Self-modifying code
- Packing/encryption
- Indirect jumps and opaque predicates
- Runtime string decoding

If you want, I can also show a more advanced version with runtime decoding and indirect calls.
